From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Norman Herms <dockseed@proton.me>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 13:53:15 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] drm/sun4i: sun60i_de_top: Map blender port 1 to VI channel 1

The top block of the A733 display engine holds, per display, a map from
the blender input ports to the channels, 4 bits per port, VI channel n
as n and UI channel n as n + 8. Its reset value 0 feeds VI channel 0
into every port. The mixer routes the layer with index n to blender
port n, so a second layer on VI channel 1 needs port 1 mapped to it.
Without this, a layer on VI channel 1 that is programmed, enabled and
routed to pipe 1 does not show, because port 1 still carries VI
channel 0.

Write the map of display 0 at probe: port 0 to VI channel 0, port 1 to
VI channel 1, as the H616 DE33 clock driver writes an undocumented word
in the same encoding at probe.

The register is not in the manual: user manual V1.00 section 14.3
(pages 641 and 642) describes the display engine without a register
table. Offset, name and field layout are the vendor's. Seen on the
Radxa Cubie A7S by register writes alone: with the map at its reset
value 0 the frame CRC reported by the sink does not change when pipe 1
is enabled with VI channel 1 behind it; with port 1 mapped to VI
channel 1 it toggles between two stable values with the pipe enable
and follows the layer coordinates.

Tested on Radxa Cubie A7S: the map reads back as written after the
first mixer bind and after a mixer unbind and bind, and a layer on VI
channel 1 shows over the primary plane.

Assisted-by: LLM
---
 drivers/gpu/drm/sun4i/sun60i_de_top.c | 18 +++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 17 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/sun4i/sun60i_de_top.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/sun4i/sun60i_de_top.c
index c9401005c13c..eb9c226e443f 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/sun4i/sun60i_de_top.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/sun4i/sun60i_de_top.c
@@ -7,7 +7,8 @@
  * display engine's MBUS port and the mux that connects each display to
  * a TCON. It is a reset and clock provider for the mixers and sets the
  * DE-to-TCON route for the TCON driver, as the TCON TOP does on the R40
- * and H6.
+ * and H6. It also holds the map from the blender input ports of each
+ * display to the channels, which is set once at probe.
  *
  * The user manual does not document these registers. Their names, bit
  * positions and the 0xf "not connected" code of the mux are the
@@ -48,6 +49,13 @@
 #define DE_TOP_DE2TCON_NONE		0xf
 #define DE_TOP_DISP_NUM			4
 
+/*
+ * vendor name: PORT2CHN_MUX, one register per display, 4 bits per
+ * blender input port: VI channel n is n, UI channel n is n + 8
+ */
+#define DE_TOP_PORT2CHN_MUX_REG(disp)	(0x30 + (disp) * 4)
+#define DE_TOP_PORT2CHN(port, chn)	((chn) << ((port) * 4))
+
 #define DE_TOP_CLK_NUM			(CLK_DE_TOP_MBUS + 1)
 #define DE_TOP_RESET_NUM		(RST_DE_TOP_MBUS + 1)
 
@@ -374,6 +382,14 @@ static int sun60i_de_top_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 				  DE_TOP_RESET_CLK_BIT16,
 				  DE_TOP_RESET_CLK_BIT16);
 
+	/*
+	 * Its reset value 0 feeds VI channel 0 into every blender port of
+	 * display 0. The mixer routes the layer with index n to port n and
+	 * gives index 1 to VI channel 1, so port 1 is VI channel 1.
+	 */
+	writel(DE_TOP_PORT2CHN(0, 0) | DE_TOP_PORT2CHN(1, 1),
+	       top->regs + DE_TOP_PORT2CHN_MUX_REG(0));
+
 	ret = sun60i_de_top_register_gates(dev, top);
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;
